        :description      "The Neurofibromatosis Therapeutic Consortium (NFTC) brought together four labs to find novel targets for clinical trials. This three-year, $4 million collaboration was co-funded with NTAP (Neurofibromatosis Therapeutic Acceleration Program) and was an extension of the Neurofibromatosis Preclinical Consortium (NFPC), a five-year, $7 million program that concluded in 2013. These two consortia cooperated to perform and complete 111 preclinical trials across different models. These studies identified 12 drug targets with promising efficacy, and 16 clinical trials have occurred based on the veracious preclinical data gathered by NFPC and NFTC investigators.  This Portal study includes the data generated by the Brigham and Women's Hospital arm of the NFTC." .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268689>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532271> ;
        :description         "LZTR1 is a substrate adaptor protein of the CUL3 ubiquitin ligase complex whose substrates and cellular functions remain mostly unknown. Our laboratory reported for the first time that human brain tumors harbor somatic mutations of the LZTR1 gene. Later studies revealed that LZTR1 is somatically mutated across many types of human tumors. Furthermore, mutations of LZTR1 were identified as the causal event leading to a significant number of cases of schwannomatosis. Schwannomatosis is a rare form of Neurofibromatosis that has only recently been identified. The genetic disorder affects less than 1 in 40,000 people, and causes the development of schwannomas on cranial, spinal and peripheral nerves. Schwannomas may cause pain that may be hard to manage. Although the causal tumor suppressor role of LZTR1 as genetic basis of schwannomatosis has been firmly established, the mechanism by which loss of the activity of the LZTR1 ubiquitin ligases triggers tumor development remains elusive. Temporally coordinated destruction of key cell regulatory proteins by the Ubiquitin-Proteasome System (UPS) represents an important control mechanism to ensure that specific protein functions are turned off at the right time, in the right compartment, and in a unidirectional fashion. Cullin/Ring/Ligase complexes (CRL) control the proteolysis of many important cell regulators. In humans, there are approximately 200 CRL ligases, each characterized by a different substrate recognition subunit that provides specificity by directly recruiting the substrates. Cullin-3 is the molecular scaffold of a major class of CRLs that use BTB domain proteins as substrate-specific adaptors. Despite their large number, only a handful of BTB domain proteins have well-established substrates and their biological functions are largely unknown. Recently, RAS and Rit1 have been proposed as potential substrates of LZTR1. In the context of this work, it was suggested that LZTR1 may inhibit MAPK pathway by repressing RAS activity, but the mechanism of RAS inhibition remains controversial. Furthermore, the full scope of LZTR1 substrates and cellular functions remain unknown.  Here, we will elucidate the normal biochemical and biological functions of LZTR1 and substrates and the mechanisms by which LZTR1 suppresses tumor growth. This work will use state of the art proteomics to identify and demonstrate the significance of crucial LZTR1 substrates in the context of cancer cells driven by LZTR1 inactivation. We anticipate that the proteomic-based discovery of new LZTR1 substrates will provide a major advance in our understanding of the links between ubiquitin ligases, tumor suppression and of the pathogenesis of Schwannomatosis and the many sporadic tumors driven by loss-of-function genetic alterations (mutations and deletions) of LZTR1. We postulate that LZTR1 substrates implement oncogenic functions that are normally restrained by LZTR1-mediated ubiquitin-dependent degradation. The expected overall impact of this innovative grant application is that it will fundamentally advance our mechanistic understanding of the LZTR1 ubiquitin ligase as genetic driver of schwannomatosis and sporadic tumors and lay the foundation for the optimization of new therapeutic strategies in syndromic and sporadic tumors associated with loss-of-function genetic alterations of LZTR1." ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268745> ;
