<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22266552>
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn8016577> ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :institutions        "University of Padua" ;
        :studyLeads          "Francesco Ciscato" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://www.doi.org/10.48105/pc.gr.93156> ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268725> ;
        :studyName           "Hexokinase 2 displacement from mitochondria-associated membranes as a new antineoplastic approach in NF1-related tumors" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :description         "The metabolic rewiring of NF1-related tumor types is emerging as an important feature of their development and progression towards malignancy, but it remains poorly described, hampering the possibility to develop selective and innovative anti-neoplastic approaches. I have recently focused my attention on a key component of the metabolic machinery of tumor cells, hexokinase 2 (HK2). HK2 induction in diverse neoplastic models is well-characterized, as well as its correlation with disease progression, malignancy and resistance to chemotherapeutics. I have recently developed an anti-neoplastic approach targeting HK2, which efficiently and specifically kills tumor cells by displacing HK2 from the interface between endoplasmic reticulum and mitochondria, where I have found HK2 localizes in neoplastic cells. This strategy is based on the use of a modular and flexible cell penetrating peptide, which is effective both in vitro and in vivo. In the present project, I am planning to specifically targeting HK2 in PN and MPNST cell models, which constitutes per se an unprecedented approach. To this aim, I will carefully evaluate the effect of the peptide and of peptide variants tailored on the features of the tumor and of its intracellular environment (e.g., extracellular protease composition) on the neoplastic growth in different tumor models, encompassing PN and MPNST cells, both in vitro and after xenograft injection in nude mice, and genetic mouse models of MPNST development." ;
        :initiative          "Young Investigator Award" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267323>
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :grantDOI            <https://www.doi.org/10.48105/pc.gr.93153> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26241781> ;
        :description         "Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors (MPNSTs) are aggressive, highly metastatic sarcomas that are in desperate need of new therapeutic approaches. Loss-of-function mutations in the histone methyltransferase Polycomb Repressive complex 2 (PRC2) are found in 60% of MPNSTs. Cancer-specific metabolic vulnerabilities represent unique therapeutic targets, and several metabolic inhibitors are entering clinical trials. However, MPNST metabolism is not well-studied, and the impact of PRC2 loss on MPNST metabolism is unknown. Given the potential benefit of targeting metabolic pathways in cancer and the increasing emphasis on metabolic inhibitors in treating NF1-deficient tumors, there is a critical need to explore the impact of PRC2 loss on MPNST metabolism. The overall goal of this project is to define the unique metabolic pathways that are disrupted in MPNSTs and to determine if these are actionable clinical targets. Our metabolomic profiling data identified the metabolite glutamine as significantly upregulated in PRC2-deleted MPNST cells. Additional studies demonstrate that treatment with the glutaminase inhibitor, CB-839, can attenuate growth of MPSNT cells with wildtype PRC2, but can accelerate growth of MPNST cells with PRC2 mutations. This data leads us to hypothesize that PRC2 loss is rewiring glutamine metabolism in MPNSTs. In Aim 1, we will examine glutamine metabolism pathways through in vitro growth assays with varying levels of key metabolic fuel sources. We will also analyze PRC2-dependent metabolic functions, including mitochondrial respiration and glycolysis using Seahorse assays and metabolite GC-MS tracing studies with metabolic inhibitors. In Aim 2, we will utilize our syngeneic orthotopic allograft MPNST model to determine the impact of PRC2 loss on in vivo efficacy of the glutaminase inhibitor CB-839. We will use a combination of pharmacological tools, CRISPR-Cas9 approaches and in vivo metabolite tracing to determine metabolic resistance mechanisms. These studies will define the impact of PRC2 on MPNST metabolism and examine the in vivo efficacy and metabolomic effects of the glutaminase inhibitor, CB839." ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26347297> ;
        :initiative          "Young Investigator Award" ;
        :studyName           "Impact of PRC2 loss on Glutamine metabolism in MPNSTs" ;
        :studyLeads          "Gavin McGivney" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn11681835> ;
