<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267330>
        :studyName           "Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors Achilles Heel: Combinatorial Targeting of ERBB3 and Calcium Signaling" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268652> ;
        :description         "This proposal will investigate the role that ErbB3 and calcium-mediated signaling pathways play in regulating cellular proliferation and survival by in malignant peripheral sheath tumors (MPNSTs). At present, there are no effective treatments for MPNSTs other than radical surgical resection. After resection, recurrence typically occurs and so patients with MPNSTs have abysmal survival rates. The pathogenesis of MPNSTs is driven by Ras hyperactivation, a result of NF1 loss, and the loss of other tumor suppressor genes such as p53, PTEN and CDKN2A that negatively regulate cell proliferation/survival. Clinically targeting hyperactive Ras and its effector molecules has failed and so there is a desperate need to identify new therapeutic targets. We have focused on identifying Ras- independent and -dependent signaling pathways necessary for MPNST proliferation and survival using comprehensive genomic analyses, kinase phospho-arrays, pharmacological screens and several animal models. Our efforts have identified ErbB3 (a receptor tyrosine kinase/RTK) and calmodulin (CaM) as potential therapeutic targets in MPNSTs. Although there is conflicting evidence that other RTKs can drive Ras hyperactivation in MPNSTs, the role of ErbB3 has not yet been investigated in MPNSTs. Interestingly, our transcriptomic analyses of NRG1? (an ErbB3 ligand) stimulated Schwann cells and our global shRNA screens implicate ErbB3 and calmodulin-mediated calcium signaling as key biological pathways driving MPNST proliferation. With the cumulative support of our screens and functional analyses, we hypothesize that simultaneous inhibition of NRG1? activated effector molecules and calmodulin regulated signaling pathways will cooperatively inhibit MPNST cellular proliferation and survival. In Aim 1, I will test the hypothesis that NRG1? activation of ErbB3 directly modulates calmodulin regulated signaling in MPNSTs to promote cellular proliferation and survival. In Aim 2, I will test the hypothesis that dual inhibition of the NRG1? effector, ErbB3, and calmodulin effector molecules induces a cytotoxic effect in MPNSTs. This work will yield novel information about MPNST tumorigenesis by investigating the role(s) of ErbB3 (an upstream activator of Ras) and calcium signaling pathways (which can be dependent or independent of Ras). My ultimate goal is to identify a novel combinatorial treatment for MPNSTs using FDA approved drugs or drugs that are already in clinical trials that can rapidly advance to clinical trials." ;
