        :institutions        "Stanford University" ;
        :studyName           "Mapping and Identifying the Roles of Protein Glycosylation in Neurofibromatosis 1" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn8012530> ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :description         "Neurofibromin is a regulator of cancer driver proteins through its functional role as a Ras-GTPase activating protein. Upon loss-of-function mutations to neurofibromin, patients develop benign or malignant nerve sheath tumors that lead to the disease neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1), affecting 1 in 2,000 people. Due to the complex signaling network of neurofibromin and the cancer-driver pathways it regulates, phosphorylation post-translation modifications have been extensively studied to explain the biological mechanisms for tumor development. Protein glycosylation, specifically N-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc), is known to have significant crosstalk with phosphorylation sites in the proteome, but has never been studied in the context of a living model system for potential regulation and contribution to the pathogenesis of NF1. To better characterize glycosylation changes at various stages of NF1 malignancies, this proposal aims to develop three complimentary chemical biology approaches to create tools for the understanding of protein GlcNAcylation in cellular model systems of NF1 and MPNSTs. We will use glycoproteomics to investigate how loss of neurofibromin affects O-GlcNAc incorporation in the proteome to reveal key sites of biological regulation affected by the Ras cancer driver pathway. We will also perform protein engineering on a N-acetylglucosamine transferase V (GnT-V), known to be overexpressed in malignant tumors, to identify protein substrates in non-malignant and malignant Schwann cells. Revealing the substrate scope of GnT-V will allow the identification of biological pathways that are aberrant in malignant nerve cells due to increased glycosylation at specific glycosites. We will also biochemically investigate glycosylation marks directly on neurofibromin, to give a clearer picture of how post-translational modifications affect the interaction of neurofibromin with cancer driver proteins and affect neurofibromin localization." ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5714290> ;
