<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268652>
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267323> ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267330> ;
        :studyName           "The use of novel YAP/TEAD Hippo pathway inhibitors to target Merlin (NF2) null tumors" ;
        :initiative          "Young Investigator Award" ;
        :studyLeads          "Liyam Laraba" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26241781> ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :grantDOI            <https://www.doi.org/10.48105/pc.gr.93158> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26347297> ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25961160> ;
        :description         "Currently, surgical resection of schwannoma and meningioma tumors is the mainstay of their treatment with the associated risks of damaging neighboring nervous system tissue and the need for repeated surgeries in those with NF2. Research in Hippo pathway signaling and its role in driving tumor development has increased greatly in the last few years and the significance of this pathway has driven the development of new small molecule inhibitors to target this important signalling pathway. The novel YAP/TEAD inhibitors developed by Vivace are stable, orally available, show no apparent toxicity and demonstrate a potent effect in vitro to block the proliferation of two human tumor types, meningioma and schwannoma, both seen in individuals with NF2. In vivo, the compounds block TEAD activity in nervous system tissue, reducing TEAD expression and strongly inhibiting levels of the TEAD target CTGF in Schwann cells. The next and obvious step is to test these compounds using two well-characterized and well-used in vivo pre-clinical models for these two tumor types. Many NF2 patients will be affected by both schwannoma and meningioma tumors and the potential for the use of a single YAP/TEAD compound to treat both tumor types in an individual patient is particularly exciting. For all these reasons, we feel that this work has the potential to create a significant impact in the future treatment of both patients with NF2 as well as the large numbers of patients with sporadic schwannoma and meningioma tumours.  We propose to test the efficacy of novel Hippo pathway inhibitor to reduce the growth of Merlin-null schwannoma and meningioma tumor cells. A collaboration (established at the 2018 CTF meeting in Paris) with the company Vivace Therapeutics has allowed us to start testing in vitro the use of novel inhibitors of TEAD auto-palmitoylation to block tumour cell growth. All MTAs for their use have been signed and Vivace will support the running costs of the project outside of the salary costs provided in this grant. These inhibitors are non-toxic, can be given orally and are tolerated well in vivo, both in our work and in other Merlin-null tumor models. Analysis of TEAD protein expression in human tumour cells shows high heterogeneity in TEAD expression between different individual human tumors; the inhibitors we plan to use for our experiments will target the function of all 4 TEAD proteins. Our data in vitro using human primary schwannoma and meningioma tumor cells shows an effective block in tumor growth, even at low concentrations of 100nM and below .  Using the periostin-CRE / NF2fl/fl model of schwannoma , we have monitored cell proliferation in vivo both within DRG and within the cochlear nerve in these animals at 3 months of age and we can already see increased cellularity within the DRG tissue and cell proliferation within the cochlear nerve. Collaborations are in place to assist with both the use of the Postn-CRE-NF2fl/fl mouse model (Dr Charles Yates, Indiana University, USA) and analysis of mouse vestibulocochlear preps (Dr Dan Jagger, UCL Ear Institute, UK). Analysis of sciatic nerve tissue shows the effectiveness of one YAP/TEAD inhibitor used in vivo to reduce TEAD expression (an effect of reduced palmitoylation) together with a strong reduction of CTGF expression, a well-characterised TEAD-dependent target in cells.  The work in this proposal will also look to test whether these TEAD inibibitors may be used to target meningioma tumor growth in vivo using an orthotopic model of meningioma in the mouse. This work will be conducted in collaboration with Professor Christian Mawrin (University of Magdeburg, Germany), who is expert in such models. As described above, the proliferation of human meningioma tumour cells is also blocked by these YAP/TEAD inhibitors.  Two different YAP/TEAD inhibitors have been supplied to us by Vivace; both will block the activity of all TEAD proteins in cells and are well characterized in terms of their pharmacokinetics and lack of toxicity in vivo.  With this exciting pilot data and the use of in vivo schwannoma and meningioma mouse pre-clinical models, we will:  1. Test the effects of YAP/TEAD inhibition upon tumor growth in the PostnCRE-NF2fl/fl mouse model at 3, 5 and 9 months.  We will use different timescales of drug treatment (at 30mg/kg) followed by EdU injection and harvesting of tissue. Tissue can be analysed by EdU stain and/or H&E analysis of paraffin sections. At later timepoints, functional analysis (eg righting reflex) may be used to functionally measure vestibulocochlear function. For all experiments, as we have used previously, the PLP-GFP line will be bred onto the PostnCRE-NF2fl/fl background to allow identification of GFP-positive glial cells in our tissue analysis. This combined mouse line is already available in our new fully IVC animal facility.  2. Test the effects of YAP/TEAD inhibition upon tumor growth in an orthotopic mouse model of meningioma.  As shown in our prelimnary data, inhibition of YAP/TEAD function significantly decreases proliferation of human primary meningioma tumor cells. These compounds are also potently effective at reducing cell proliferation of meningioma cell lines as well in vitro (eg. BenMen1 and KT21 cells). Meningioma tumors are the most common intracranial tumor type and are common in patients with NF2.  We will use the well characterised convexity and skull base models to test the effectiveness of the YAP/TEAD inhibitors in blocking tumor growth. Currently genetic models of meningioma development (eg. prostaglandin D2 synthase driven CRE) do not allow for the testing of such compounds.  In these experiments we will use injection of meningioma cell lines (BenMen1, KT21) and monitor cell proliferation by EdU and analysis of samples by H&E staining with and without inhibitor dosing.  Through these two aims, this project will test whether these inhibitors may be suitable for clinical trials in the future." ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn11681835> ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_2 ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268815> ;
        :institutions        "University of Plymouth" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268639>
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn4939872> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn4939890> ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268816> ;
        :studyLeads          "Jennifer Patritti Cram" ;
        :institutions        "Cincinnati Medical Center" ;
        :description         "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is a disorder that affects 1:3,000 individuals worldwide. Loss of function of the neurofibroma gene (NF1) causes over activation of the RAS oncogenic signaling pathway. People with NF1 have an increased risk of developing tumors (neurofibromas), both benign and malignant anywhere along the peripheral nervous system (PNS). Neurofibromas arise from Schwann cells (SCs) and/or Schwann cell precursors (SCP). SCs support axons in the peripheral nervous system (PNS). To date, there is no cure for this disease. Therefore, the purpose of this proposal is to identify potential treatments for NF1 by studying the mechanisms involved in tumor formation.  To identify potential markers and therapeutic targets for NF1, we performed microarray analysis on human plexiform neurofibromas. We dissociated human neurofibromas and sorted tumor derived cells into SCs (p75+/EGFR-) and SCP tumor-initiating cells (p75+/EGFR+) (p75 marks neural crest cells, SC precursors, and non-myelinating SCs, and EGFR+ marks cells that express the Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor) [Schwann cells are EGFR negative]. The hypothesis behind the cell sorting is that genes expressed in neurofibroma SCP-tumor initiating cells (p75+/EGFR+) more than in differentiated neurofibroma Schwann cells (p75+/EGFR-) contribute to tumor initiation. From this microarray study, we found that a purinergic receptor, P2RY14 expression was increased in SCP-like tumor initiating cells (p75+/EGFR+). Then, we sorted the SCP tumor initiating cells (p75+/EGFR+) into P2RY14 positive or negative (p75+/EGFR+/P2RY14+ or p75+/EGFR+/P2RY14-) and we found that the p75+/EGFR+/P2RY14+ cells show an increased ability to self-renew and form spheres in vitro. These results suggest that tumor initiating cells (p75+/EGFR+) that are P2RY14+ might drive tumor growth.  To test if P2RY14 is tumor promoting in vivo, I bred our genetically engineered neurofibroma mouse model (Nf1 fl/fl DhhCre) to P2RY14 knockout mice (P2RY14-/-). Knocking out P2RY14 in the neurofibroma mice (P2Y14-/-; Nf1 fl/fl DhhCre) increases survival and reduces tumor number. This data demonstrates that P2RY14 is involved in neurofibroma formation and that knockout of this receptor in the neurofibroma mouse model increases survival rate in neurofibroma bearing mice.  I hypothesize that P2RY14 promotes neurofibroma growth by enhancing tumor cell self-renewal and/or proliferation in vitro and that inhibition of this receptor in vivo will result in a decrease of tumor growth. This hypothesis will be tested by completion of these specific aims:  Aim 1: Determine if the P2RY14 receptor is necessary for neurofibroma tumor cell proliferation in vitro. I hypothesize that P2RY14 signaling promotes neurofibroma growth in a cell autonomous way by regulating SCP proliferation and/or self-renewal in vitro. A) To determine if P2RY14 activation affects SCP cell proliferation, I will measure proliferating cell rates in WT and Nf1-/- SCP after addition of UDP (P2RY14 agonist). B) To determine if P2RY14 is necessary for neurofibroma SCP proliferation, I will silence P2RY14 expression in WT and Nf1-/- SCP in vitro using the P2RY14 pharmacological inhibitor and/or short-hairpin RNA (shRNA) and measure the rate of proliferating cells. If a change is found, I will determine if the change is MAPK dependent by monitoring RAS signaling or, determine if the change is P2RY14 dependent by monitoring cAMP levels.  Aim 2: Determine if the P2RY14 inhibitor drug can decrease neurofibroma growth. I will perform a drug study to determine if administration of a P2RY14 inhibitor by itself and/or in combination with the MEK inhibitor selumetinib for 7 days decreases cell proliferation, cell death, PKA and/or MAPK signaling. I will read out results by immunohistochemistry in tumor sections. If no change is found then, I will conclude that P2RY14 effects are mainly through tumor initiation. If an effect is seen, I will determine what cell types are involved in this process and increase the duration of the drug treatment to assess tumor size and tumor growth kinetics overtime.  Upon completion of the proposed aims in this grant, I will determine if P2RY14 is necessary for neurofibroma tumor cell proliferation in vitro and test if P2RY14 inhibition by drug delivery has an effect on tumor growth in vivo.  A critical gap in the field are the absence of mechanistic studies on the cause of tumor formation and the limited number of drugs available to treat NF1. Therefore, to fill this critical gap in knowledge, in Aim 1 I propose to determine if P2RY14 is necessary for neurofibroma cell proliferation in vitro. In Aim 2, I propose to determine if a P2RY14 pharmacological inhibitor can decrease neurofibroma growth.  Our results will impact the neurofibroma field not only by defining purinergic signaling mechanisms relevant to neurofibroma development but I will also establish if the P2RY14 inhibitor can be used as a treatment for NF1." ;
