        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268765> ;
        :institutions        "University of California San Francisco" ;
        :description         "Neurofibromatosis type 1 is one of the most common dominantly inherited genetic disorders, affecting approximately 1 in 3000 individuals, and is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NF1 gene product, neurofibromin 1 (NF1). The clinical manifestations of neurofibromatosis type 1 exhibit significant heterogeneity but frequently patients develop learning difficulties, bone deformities and benign tumors of Schwann cells in the skin (dermal neurofibromas) and/or peripheral nerves (plexiform neurofibromas). These tumors can cause patients significant pain, disfiguration and functional impairment. Plexiform neurofibromas can develop in to malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (MPNSTs), the most common malignancy occurring in neurofibromatosis type 1 patients, for which there are poor rates of survival and no effective approved treatments. Clearly, an understanding of NF1 biology at the molecular level is critical for the development of effective therapeutic strategies for patients with neurofibromatosis type 1.  Despite much effort over several decades, a robust molecular understanding of how NF1 is regulated is severely lacking. NF1 is as a RAS GTPase-activating protein (GAP) and negatively regulates RAS activation by stimulating hydrolysis of RAS bound GTP to GDP. Growth factors recruit guanine nucleotide exchange factors (GEFs) such as SOS to promote GTP loading of RAS, and RAS-GTP then binds to several effectors to activate critical downstream signaling cascades, including RAF-MEK1/2-ERK1/2 signaling (MAP kinase pathway). Activation of RAS under normal circumstances is transient and tightly controlled, and we know from biochemical kinetic and genetic evidence that NF1�s ability to switch off RAS must itself be actively regulated. However, despite findings from our laboratory that NF1 forms a high-affinity homodimer, and also heterodimerizes with the negative regulator of RAS signalling SPRED1, our understanding of the processes by which NF1 is regulated remains very limited.  In this proposal we now present robust evidence that NF1 is directly phosphorylated by the ERK1 and ERK2 MAP kinases. We believe this observation is an important foundation for fundamentally furthering our molecular understanding of NF1 biology. We propose biochemical and genetic approaches to test the hypothesis that these phosphorylation events are a negative feedback loop to NF1 that might be critical for the normal switching off of RAS signaling that follows its activation. This would represent the first identified negative feedback loop to RAS-GAPs. If correct, we plan to determine the mechanisms by which ERK1/2 regulate NF1 using biochemical and genetically-engineered cell models. We also propose to test whether loss of such a negative feedback mechanism that permits the inappropriate hyperactivation of ERK1/2 and other RAS effectors might underlie MPNST development in neurofibromatosis type 1 in vivo models. Loss of a RAS-ERK-NF1 feedback mechanism could also explain why MEK1/2 inhibitors (MEKi), which are under priority review by the FDA, exhibit a therapeutic window and are effective for the treatment of plexiform neurofibromas, but in most sporadic cancers with RAS- MAPK mutations exhibit a narrow therapeutic window and consequently modest efficacy. An understanding of feedback regulation of NF1 by ERK1/2 would be invaluable for our understanding of how best to employ and schedule ERK1/2 pathway inhibitors, such as MEKi, in patients with neurofibromatosis type 1. This knowledge may also inform the development of alternative therapeutic strategies. Finally, unveiling mechanisms by which ERK1/2 regulate NF1 could also reveal how other cellular signals regulate NF1, such as those anticipated from receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs)." ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn8012530> ;
        :studyName           "Unveiling the molecular mechanisms of neurofibromin regulation for therapeutic targeting in neurofibromatosis type 1" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267284> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532271> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23662493> ;
