<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn21984813>
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn21993642> ;
        :dataType            :Genomic_Variants ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn11638893> ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :description         "NF1 is one of the most prevalent cancer predisposition syndromes, affecting 1 in 2500 individuals worldwide. Approximately 10-13% of affected individuals will develop an aggressive sarcoma, the malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST). One of the major barriers to the improving outcomes for these patients is the lack of preclinical models that accurately represent the genetic heterogeneity of these cancers. In this regard, no effective therapies have emerged from recent clinical trials, which likely reflects the use of a single mouse MPNST model system (Nf1+/-;Trp53+/- mice) and a paucity of human tumor xenografts. To address this critical problem, our laboratories have successfully generated a series of patient-derived MPNST xenografts that more accurately reflect the genetic heterogeneity seen in the human condition. To date, we have generated 15 NF1-MPNST patient-derived xenograft (PDX) lines propagated in immunocompromised mice. We have demonstrated that these lines harbor the spectrum of genomic alterations that we see in patients with NF1, including germline and somatic NF1 mutations, and subsets with loss of CDKN2A, TP53 mutations, EED/SUZ12 mutations, and numerous copy number changes. We plan to use collagen microtissues generated from the PDX lines in vitro to perform high throughput drug screening, to accelerate identification of efficacious therapeutic combinations. We will then proceed with in vivo testing of the promising combinations. This proposal brings together four institutions with deep expertise in NF1 research and a non-profit, collaborative research institute, Sage Bionetworks, to develop a truly integrated effort to explore the therapeutic potential of a set of well-vetted drugs for MPNST. Specific Scientific Aims Aim 1: Continue development and characterization of NF1-MPNST PDX lines to maintain the platform for pre-clinical testing. Our goal is to generate a total of at least 20 unique PDX lines available for use in Aims 2 and 3. Aim 2: Determine the in vitro response to cytotoxic chemotherapy or targeted therapy based on molecular phenotype and computational modeling. Our goal is to screen all PDX for responses to a set of well vetted candidate drugs and drug combinations using a novel high throughput 3-dimensional tissue culture platform. Aim 3: Determine the in vivo response to cytotoxic chemotherapy or targeted therapy or their combination and identify molecular signatures that are associated with this response." ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :institutions        "Washington University in St. Louis" ;
        :studyName           "Predictive Modeling Utilizing a Preclinical NF1-MPNST Platform Suitable for Precision Oncology" ;
        :dataType            :Drug_Screen ;
        :fundingAgency       "NFRI|NIH-NCI" ;
