<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532255>
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :studyLeads          "Andrea Rasola" ;
        :studyName           "TRAPping neurofibromas. Inhibition of the mitochondrial chaperone TRAP1 as an anti-neoplastic strategy for NF1-associated tumors" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532336> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn8016650> ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :initiative          "Drug Discovery Initiative Registered Reports" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://www.doi.org/10.48105/pc.gr.141322> ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :manifestation       :Plexiform_Neurofibroma ;
        :description         "Plexiform neurofibroma (PN) is a complex tumor that develops within perineural sheaths of nerve bundles in patients with neurofibromatosis type I (NF1), a tumor-predisposing genetic syndrome. Albeit benign, PNs can be disfiguring, can dangerously compress surrounding tissues or undergo transformation into highly aggressive sarcomas termed malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (MPNSTs). PN formation and growth require the deregulated induction in Schwann cells of the Ras/ERK transduction axis that follows homozygous loss-of-function mutations at the NF1 gene, which encodes the Ras GTPase activating protein neurofibromin.Oncogenic signalling via the Ras/ERK pathway contributes to the metabolic rewiring of neoplastic cells, and mitochondria can play a promoter role in these changes, allowing cells to thrive in the conditions of limited oxygen supply they encounter during the neoplasm accrual. We have previously shown that Ras/ERK signalling has a mitochondrial branch in tumor cells. In NF1-related tumor cell models, hyperactive mitochondrial ERK enhances the activity of the molecular chaperone TRAP1. We have demonstrated that TRAP1 causes inhibition of succinate dehydrogenase (SDH), an enzyme placed at the crossroad between Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS), increasing intracellular levels of the oncometabolite succinate, which in turn activates the pro-neoplastic programs of the transcription factor HIF1. Therefore, TRAP1 contributes to the pro-neoplastic metabolic rewiring of NF1-related tumor cells, and its genetic ablation abolishes tumorigenicity of MPNST cells in xenograft models.Taken together, these data indicate that TRAP1 is an interesting target for the development of anti-neoplastic compounds active on PNs and MPNSTs. In the framework of a previous CTF Drug Discovery Initiative, we have used innovative molecular dynamics approaches to identify pharmacophore pockets in the TRAP1 structure that can host small molecules with a potential activity as allosteric inhibitors of TRAP1 chaperone cycle. We have selected eleven compounds that inhibit TRAP1 activity in a very specific way, as they are not effective on its cognate chaperone Hsp90. These molecules revert TRAP1-dependent inhibition of SDH in MPNST cells and abrogate their in vitro tumorigenicity.Based on this solid ground of preliminary data, our present project aims at the in vivo assessment of the anti-neoplastic effect of TRAP1 inhibition. We will use a double-pronged approach: first, we will analyze wheter TRAP1-targeting compounds affect the xenografic growth of PN and MPNST cell models in nude mice; second, we will exploit a genetic mouse model of MPNST onset and growth, the Nf1flox/flox; p53flox/flox; PLP-CreERT2+ mouse in which both the Nf1 and the p53 locus can be selectively ablated in Schwann cell precursors, to investigate whether TRAP1 allosteric inhibitors can block or even revert the growth of these malignancies. We will then perform ex vivo experiments on tumor samples in order to determine their metabolic and biochemical features, and how TRAP1 blockade impacts on them.In collaboration with Giorgio Colombo, University of Pavia, Italy, we will also expand the panel of TRAP1 allosteric inhibitors. To this aim, we will utilize a molecular dynamics approach to to predict molecular scaffold derivatives of our panel of inhibitors that could display an increased inhibitory effect on TRAP1 chaperone and pro-neoplastic activity.Future developments of this proposal foresee the combined use of TRAP1 allosteric inhibitors with other anti-neurofibroma drugs, e.g. molecules targeting the Ras/ERK signalling pathway.Altogether, the experiments of the present application are intended to exploit the metabolic liabilities of PNs and MPNSTs, in order to extend the weaponry that can be used against these malignancies. TRAP1 allosteric inhibitors could constitute an innovative approach for the development of selective and effective anti-tumor drugs for NF1 patients." .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532258>
        :initiative          "Drug Discovery Initiative Registered Reports" ;
        :description         "Background: Patients with Schwannomatosis overwhelmingly present with intractable pain. The etiology of pain in Schwannomatosis is not clear, and there are no effective therapies that relieve Schwannomatosis pain. Schwannomatosis research is limited by the lack of established Schwannomatosis cell lines and animal models. Malignant cancer cells can grow indefinitely in cell culture and can form xenograft tumors in mice, which allows for the characterization of their growth and assessment of novel therapeutic drugs. However, as a benign tumor, Schwannomatosis tissues isolated from patients do not grow in culture. Therefore, developing a platform to study tumor-induced pain and identify novel strategies that relieve pain in patients with Schwannomatosis is a significant unmet need. In our preliminary studies, we have established patient-derived Schwannomatosis cell lines.By culturing Schwannomatosis cells in the DRG model, we found that Schwannomatosis cells secrete molecules that activate macrophages, which produce inflammatory cytokines and induce pain responses in the dorsal root ganglia. We performed RNASeq gene expression profiling study, and found that inflammatory pathways are activated and genes in the NRG-1/ErbB pathways are elevated in Schwannomas. We further found that schwannomas, via producing NRG1, activates ErbB receptor on tumor-associated macrophages to release inflammatory cytokines, and thus contributing to the development of neuropathic pain.Hypothesis: We hypothesis that Schwannomatosis tumor cells, via producing elevated levels of NRG-1, activate ErbB receptor on tumor-associated macrophages to induce inflammatory pain.Specific Aims: We propose to determine in:    Aim 1) determine the mechanisms of NRG1/ErbB signaling on pain response and tumor growth in DRG culture model;    Aim 2) determine the efficacy of NRG1/ErbB blockade on controlling pain and tumor growth in Schwannomatosis mouse model.Study Design: In Aim 1, we will use wide-spectrum ErbB tyrosine kinase inhibitors to block NRG-1/ErbB signaling in macrophages, and determine its effects on pain molecule expression in DRG slice culture model. In Aim 2, we will implant Schwannomatosis cells in the sciatic nerve model. We will treat mice with ErbB TKI to block NRG1/ErbB signaling, and evaluate its effects on tumor growth and pain response." ;
