<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532271>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22266606> ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5714288> ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532343> ;
        :description         "Plexiform neurofibromas (PNs) are complex, precancerous, slow-growing tumors arising from large peripheral and cranial nerves in pateitns with neurofibromatsosi type 1 (NF1) and can cause severe morbidity including paralysis and death through their progressive compression of vital structures such as the spinal cord, the airway and vasculature. Currently, there is only one approved drug therapy (selumetinib) to treat plexiform neurofibromas in NF1 patients, and a significant subset of patients do not respond. Thus, novel therapeutic strategies to treat PNs are urgently needed. NF1 is caused by germline mutations in the NF1 tumor suppressor, which encodes a GTPase-activating protein (GAP) called neurofibromin that forms a molecular complex with activated Ras-GTP and increases Ras-GTP hydrolysis to the inactive GDP-bound conformation. When neurofibromin function is disrupted by genetic mutations, Ras activity become aberrantly increased, leading to tumorigenesis. Ras is at the apex of a series of signaling cascades, including the Ras-Raf-Mek-Erk, PI3K, and Rho pathways. Given the complexity of the downstream signaling pathways, the most ideal treatment strategy for NF1 is to reduce pathological activation of Ras signaling itself. However, due to its complex post translational modification and small binding pocket, it has been long regarded as a pharmacologically intractable target. Recently, however, next generation small molecular Rac inhibitors have entered development and have demonstrated considerable promise. PSC5-6 is a small molecular Ras inhibitor developed by Pisces Therapeutics, which has demonstrated activity in NF1 sarcoma (malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor) cell lines and additionally, in an animal model of another closely linked <U+FFFD>Rasopathy, <U+FFFD>Costell Syndrome. In this study, we will evaluate the tolerability, pharmacokinetic, and pharmacodynamic properties of PSC5-6 in a genetically engineered mouse model of NF1, and further, whether PSC5-6 can halt and/or prevent the progression of plexiform neurofibromas in a genetically engineered mouse model of NF1. PSC5-6 proves efficacious in this preclinical model, these studies will generate the data needed to catalyze the clinical translation of PSDC5-6 in an early phase trial in NF1 patients with plexifom neurofibroma who are refractory to currently available treatment modalities." ;
