<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532273>
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :studyName           "Exploiting macropinocytosis for therapeutic delivery to Nf2-deficient schwannoma cells" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://www.doi.org/10.48105/pc.gr.141558> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26383473> ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26238842> ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :initiative          "Drug Discovery Initiative Registered Reports" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :studyLeads          "Jeremie Vitte" ;
        :manifestation       :Schwannoma ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn2343195> ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_2 ;
        :description         "Significance/RationaleThe benign, slow-growing nature of schwannomas presents unique challenges to therapeutic intervention, precluding the use of strategies that target rapidly dividing, metabolically active cells, and diminishing the therapeutic window for treatment. Recent work from the McClatchey lab, showed that merlin deficiency noticeably primes the membrane:cytoskeleton interface for growth factor-induced macropinocytosis, including in schwannoma cells. Mounting evidence suggests that beyond its physiological roles, macropinocytosis is also an important conduit for the delivery of therapeutic macromolecules into tumor cells. Since target cell entry is rate-limiting for many macro-therapeutics, tumor cells that are macropinocytosis-proficient may be particularly amenable to these promising strategies. Moreover, once internalized, macropinosomes merge with the endolysosomal system, and it is generally thought that slowing or impeding endolysosomal processing protects the contents from lysosomal degradation and enhances macro-therapeutic efficacy. The goal of this proposal is to evaluate the in vivo uptake of stapled peptides by Nf2-/- schwannoma cells via macropinocytosis. We will monitor the ability of Nf2-/- cells to internalize two fluorescently stapled peptides in vivo: one that mimics the BH3-domain of pro-apoptotic proteins to inhibit BCL2 family members and one that inhibits histone deacetylase (HDAC) family members. In addition to being the lead examples of this type of therapeutic strategy, both peptides have been tested in mouse models and BH3 mimetics have been extensively used in human clinical trials.Preliminary DataTo demonstrate that schwannoma cells of PeriostinCre;Nf2flox/flox DRG are primed for macropinocytosis, we performed an in vivo macropinocytosis uptake assay by intravenously (i.v.) injecting the fluorescently-tagged macro molecule FITC-Ficoll (70 kDa). DRGs were then dissected and further incubated in cell culture plates with the fluorescently-tagged TMR-Dextran. This two-step assay demonstrated that the Nf2-deficient cells in PeriostinCre;Nf2flox/flox DRGs are uptaking macromolecules from the extracellular medium and importantly that macromolecules injected intravenously are able to reach the DRG schwannomas.Aims    Specific Aim 1. To demonstrate specific macropinocytosis uptake in Nf2-deficient cells in vivo. We will test the hypothesis that Nf2-deficient vs normal cells preferentially take up stapled peptides in vivo via macropinocytosis. We will i.v. inject Nf2flox/flox control and Postn-Cre;Nf2flox/flox mutant mice with FITC-Ficoll to reveal cellular areas of macropinocytosis uptake. Comparison between Nf2-deficient tumor cells in DRG and Nf2-epxressing tissues like normal nerve roots or sciatic nerve will allow to demonstrate if the macropinocytosis uptake is specific to Nf2-deficient tumor cells.    Specific Aim 2. To optimize the delivery route and concentration of hydrocarbon stapled peptides to reach DRG schwannoma cells of Postn-Cre;Nf2flox/flox mice. We will test different dosages of fluorescently-tagged peptides (BIM-FITC and 16cyc-HxA-FITC) and two different delivery routes (intraperitoneal and tail vein injection) to evaluate the biodistribution of the stapled peptides in the Nf2-deficient cells of the DRGs and in Nf2-expressing tissues.    Specific Aim 3. To evaluate the efficacy of hydrocarbon stapled peptides in controlling the schwannoma cells proliferation in Postn-Cre;Nf2flox/flox mice. In this aim we will assess the effect of the two hydrocarbon stapled peptides (BIM and 16cyc-HxA) on tumor growth in the Postn-Cre;Nf2flox/flox mice.PerspectivesSuccessful completion of this studies will establish a new ex vivo assay for high throughput screening of drug compounds inhibiting macropinocytosis uptake in Nf2-deficient cells. Importantly, this ex vivo assay can be performed with both mouse and human schwannoma tissues. Complementary ex vivo analyses in human schwannoma tissue will help to further refine and optimize these novel strategies in advance of clinical testing. This proposal will provide a proof-of-concept for the use of two hydrocarbon stapled peptides and further studies will be able to test other macro-therapeutics (protein-conjugates, lipid nanoparticules, extravesicular vesicles or synthetic nanocarriers and exosomes based on the same model that NF2-deficient tumor cells and not normal cells are primed for macromolecules uptake." ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532344> ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23639889>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn17098100> ;
        :manifestation       :Unknown_Annotation ;
        :initiative          "NFRP New Investigator Award" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23651229> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5714289> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn7217935> ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23651006> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999248> ;
        :studyLeads          "Angela Hirbe" ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :fundingAgency       "CDMRP" ;
        :studyName           "TYK2 as a Biomarker and Therapeutic Target for NF1-Associated Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors" ;
        :description         "The most common malignant cancer affecting people with neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is the malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST), a highly aggressive soft tissue cancer (sarcoma) that commonly develops from a benign precursor tumor. In this regard, 8%-13% of adults with NF1 will develop MPNSTs. Currently, there are no effective treatments for these cancers, and the vast majority of people with MPNST will die within 5 years of initial diagnosis.  Because these cancers arise from benign tumors, there is also a pressing need to be able to diagnose and distinguish true MPNSTs from non-cancerous precursors, which appear similar on advanced imaging studies such as MRI or PET scans. Using DNA sequencing technologies, we recently identified a frequently mutated gene in NF1-associated MPNSTs called TYK2. This mutation is not found in the benign precursors, however. Furthermore, in our previous experiments, we showed that mutations in this gene are critical for tumor cell survival, both in petri dishes and in tumors from mice.  Thus, we propose that TYK2 mutations drive tumor growth by preventing cell death. In this manner, TYK2 mutations may suggest a poor prognosis for those MPNSTs that harbor them. Furthermore, drugs that block the effects of TYK2 may prove to be useful in the battle against this aggressive cancer.  In this project, we aim to clarify the role of TYK2 in MPNST development and growth by performing experiments in previously engineered tumor cell lines in petri dishes as well as brand new tumor cells derived by our laboratory from patient samples and grown in mice. In addition, we will determine whether TYK2 mutations can reliably determine the likelihood of a poor prognosis or future tumor progression. Finally, we will assess whether drugs that block TYK2 prevent MPNST growth both in the petri dish and in mouse tumors. These experiments have the potential to lead to a markedly better treatment, which is desperately needed for these aggressive sarcomas." ;
        :institutions        "Washington University in St. Louis" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23662493>
        :initiative          "Open Proposal Program" ;
        :dataType            : ;
        :fundingAgency       "NTAP" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5714290> ;
