        :studyName           "Developing ELISA/IHC-Capable NF1 Monooclonal Antibody" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :institutions        "Infixion Bioscience, Inc." ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22266606> ;
        :dataType            :Gene_Expression ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532271> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn8012530> ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23662497> ;
        :dataType            :Proteomics ;
        :description         "Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1) is an autosomal dominant genetic disorder in which the amount of wild type neurofibromin produced by the remaining functional gene is a significant factor in the progression and severity of NF1 symptoms (i.e., haploinsufficiency driven). Given neurofibromin’s impact in regulating the Ras pathway and the importance of neurofibromin protein levels directly impacting many downstream pathways the availability of sensitive and specific antibodies to facilitate protein measurement becomes critically important.  Unfortunately, commercially available NF1 antibodies (both polyclonal and monoclonal) have been shown to have significant limitations, including the inability to use these antibodies in ELISA and IHC applications. Utilizing full-length neurofibromin (isolated and purified by the Frank McCormick lab and Dominic Esposito’s team at the Frederick National Lab) as the immunogen, and leveraging a proprietary antibody development platform, Alicanto©, from Digital Proteomics, LLC, the San Diego-based team at Infixion Bioscience set out to develop a new and improved monoclonal antibody against NF1. Project goals include:      identifying a mAb with increased sensitivity and specificity against neurofibromin as determined by western blot, and     demonstrate expanded utility with function in ELISAs, sandwich ELISAs and immunohistochemistry (IHC) applications.  Additional collaborating partners on the project included: Dr. Michael Frost (IHC development), Dr. Dominic Esposito (epitope mapping), and Dr. Eduard Serra Lab (characterization in NF1 iPSC lines)." .

