        :diseaseFocus               :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :dataType                   :Clinical_Data ;
        :studyLeads                 "Angela Hirbe" ;
        :relatedStudies             <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn7217935> ;
        :accessRequirements         "Accessing data from this project requires submission of an Intended Data Use Statement and agreement to Data Access Terms of Use.  <br><br> To request access to data from this project, please follow the steps outlined [here](https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23651229/wiki/612595)." ;
        :relatedStudies             <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5714289> ;
        :studyFileviewId            <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23651255> ;
        :acknowledgementStatements  "If you publish work using data from this project, please: </br> <ul> <li>Cite the primary publication: Szymanski JJ, Sundby RT, Jones PA, Srihari D, Earland N, Harris PK, et al. (2021) Cellfree DNA ultra-low- pass whole genome sequencing to distinguish malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST) from its benign precursor lesion: A cross-sectional study. PLoS Med 18(8): e1003734. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1003734 </li> <li>Acknowledge the NF Data Portal using the following statement: \"The results published here are in whole or in part based on data obtained from the NF Data Portal http://www.nfdataportal.org and made available through the NF Open Science Initiative.\"</li> </ul>" ;
        :description                "The leading cause of mortality for patients with the Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) cancer predisposition syndrome is development of Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumor (MPNST), an aggressive soft tissue sarcoma. In the setting of NF1, this aggressive cancer frequently arises in histologically benign plexiform neurofibroma (PN). However, transformation from the benign PN to MPNST can be challenging to diagnose due to equivocal imaging results and intralesional heterogeneity resulting in biopsy sampling errors. This multi-institution study used ultra-low-pass whole genome sequencing (ULP-WGS) of plasma cell-free DNA (cfDNA) to distinguish between MPNST and PN neoplasms in patients with NF1." ;
        :studyLeads                 "Jack Shern" ;
        :fundingAgency              "NIH-NCI| Children’s Cancer Foundation| Cancer Research Foundation| The V Foundation for Cancer Research| Center for Cancer Research|NTAP" ;
        :studyName                  "Ultra-low-pass whole genome sequencing of plasma cfDNA from patients with plexiform neurofibroma and malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors" ;
        :relatedStudies             <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999248> ;
        :manifestation              :Unknown_Annotation .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23664726>
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn4939908> ;
        :fundingAgency       "NTAP" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :institutions        "Johns Hopkins University" ;
        :studyLeads          "Kavita Sarin" ;
        :manifestation       :Cutaneous_Neurofibroma ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23664752> ;
        :description         "The manifestations of NF1 are highly variable between affected individuals. Greater than 2800 pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene are associated with NF1, however, only a very small number of NF1 variants have been associated with a predictable phenotype. These associations include a severe disease burden in the case of a constitutional NF1 microdeletion and missense mutations of codons 844 to 848 versus milder manifestations associated with missense mutation codon Arg 1809 or a single amino acid deletion at position 2971.1,2 These mutations occur in an exceedingly rare number of patients, but illustrate the potential value of genotype-phenotype discovery for both clinical care (re: prognosis and recommended surveillance) as well as in selecting patient populations most likely to benefit from various therapeutic approaches in development.  Multiple challenges have contributed to the limited number of identified genotype-phenotype correlations. These include the age dependency of many manifestations in NF1, the timing and number of second hits in different cells, and wide NF1 allelic heterogeneity. Advances in understanding the relationship between genotype and clinical manifestations is critical to personalizing therapeutic development for both individual manifestations of NF1 and optimizing clinical management of genetically classified subpopulations of people with NF1. This proposal seeks to investigate modifier genes that are present in adults with NF1 with high versus low burden of cutaneous neurofibromas (cNF). Our overall goal is to evaluate the genetic basis of the variability in burden of cNF in people with NF1 in order to facilitate new therapeutic development for cNF and advancing the understanding about the variability of NF1." ;
        :studyLeads          "Carlos G. Romo" ;
        :initiative          "Open Proposal Program" ;
        :studyLeads          "Frank McCormick" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://doi.org/10.54464/pc.gr.147836> ;
        :institutions        "University of California, San Francisco" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :institutions        "Stanford Hospital and Clinics" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :studyLeads          "Jaishri Blakeley" ;
        :studyName           "Evaluating genetic modifiers of cutaneous neurofibromas in adults with Neurofibromatosis Type 1" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn24875850>
        :manifestation              :Schwannoma ;
        :dataType                   :Chromatin_Activity ;
        :initiative                 "Opening the Data Drawers" ;
        :institutions               "University Health Network of Toronto" ;
        :diseaseFocus               :Schwannomatosis ;
        :fundingAgency              "CTF" ;
        :studyLeads                 "Gelareh Zadeh" ;
        :studyStatus                "Completed" ;
        :description                "Aberrant DNA methylation is associated with cancer development and progression. There are several types of specimens from which DNA methylation patterns can be measured and evaluated as an indicator of disease status (from normal biological process to pathologic condition) and even of pharmacologic response to therapy. Blood-based specimens such as cell-free circulating nucleic acid and DNA extracted from leukocytes in peripheral blood may be a potential source of non-invasive cancer biomarkers. As part of our synodos for schwannomatosis study we focused on multiplatform genomic analysis of schwannomatosis related schwannomas. We also performed DNA methylation profiling on whole genomic DNA isolated from peripheral blood leukocytes from 27 schwannomatosis patients and 22 patients with sporadic non-syndromic schwannomas. This analysis was outside the scope of the synodos for schwannomatosis study; however, we were interested in evaluating the proportion of immune cell types in the blood of patients with peripheral nerve sheath tumors including schwannomatosis patients. After performing QC analysis, we removed 5 schwannomatosis samples (failed QC: detp>0.05). We then Removed 176834 failed probes (detp>0.05), 12613 probes on the X and Y sex chromosomes, 23507 probes associated with SNPs at the CpG sites, and 24267 cross-reactive probes. Total 630553 probes were left for clustering analysis. We then performed cell deconvolution analysis using IDOLOptimizedCpGs method  to determine the proportion of immune cell types in blood of patients with schwannomatosis or non-syndromic sporadic schwannomas including neutrophils, monocytes, CD4 T cells, CD8 T cells, B cells and NK cells. We then clustered the samples based on immune cell proportions. We found that schwannomatosis samples clustered separate from sporadic schwannomas. Our results suggested that schwannomatosis patients harboured a higher neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio (NLR) compared to schwannomas. Further studies are required to determine the clinical implications of this result, in addition to continued work to uncover the biological underpinnings of these differences among these syndromic and non-syndromic disorders." ;
        :relatedStudies             <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23532258> ;
        :acknowledgementStatements  "You will acknowledge the NF Data Portal using the following statement: \"The results published here are in whole or in part based on data obtained from the NF Data Portal http://www.nfdataportal.org and made available through the NF Open Science Initiative.\"<br>   You promise to acknowledge the research participants as data contributors by including the following statement: “The research team would like to thank the patients who generously shared their time and tissue for the purposes of this project.”<br> You promise to cite the study investigators on all publications or presentations resulting from the use of these data as follows: Mansouri, S., Suppiah, S., Mamatjan, Y. et al. Epigenomic, genomic, and transcriptomic landscape of schwannomatosis. Acta Neuropathol (2020). https://doi.org/10.1007/s00401-020-02230-x" ;
        :dataStatus                 "Available" ;
        :accessRequirements         "Accessing data from this project requires submission of an Intended Data Use Statement and agreement to Data Access Terms of Use.  <br><br> To access data from this project, please follow the instructions here: [access request instructions](https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn24875850/wiki/613114)." ;
        :studyName                  "DNA methylation signatures in peripheral leukocytes of patients with Schwannomatosis or sporadic schwannoma" ;
        :studyFileviewId            <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn24875854> ;
        :relatedStudies             <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn9727752> ;
        :grantDOI                   <https://www.doi.org/10.48105/pc.gr.141738> ;
        rdf:type                    :Study .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn20608511>
        :studyLeads       "Gordon Harris" ;
        :description      "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the most common diagnostic imaging modality for longitudinal monitoring of patients with Neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1), NF2, and schwannomatosis. Because NF tumors can be extensive and occurring anywhere in the body, whole-body MRI (WBMRI), instead of regional MRI, is recommended as the standard imaging modality for longitudinal evaluating of NF patients. However, conventional linear tumor measurement, such as bi-diameter (WHO) or single diameter (RECIST), is less sensitive and less reliable to measure the growth rate of irregularly shaped NF tumors that grow in non-uniform ways. Hence, measurement of tumor volume is a key component in estimating tumor burden in NF patients. For NF tumor volumetrics in MRI, image segmentation can be challenging, as the lesions are irregular and complex, and there may be many lesions in a patient. Efficient and reliable tumor volumetric segmentation methods are needed for the assessment of the patient tumor burden including the number, size (volume), location (body part), and type (discrete or plexiform) of NF tumors carried by a patient." ;
        :studyStatus      "Completed" ;
        :dataType         :Image ;
        :studyFileviewId  <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn20686637> ;
        :relatedStudies   <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn4984604> ;
        :dataType         :Clinical_Data ;
        :studyLeads       "Wenli Cai" ;
        :diseaseFocus     :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :fundingAgency    "CTF" ;
        :institutions     "Massachusetts General Hospital" ;
        :initiative       "Opening the Data Drawers" ;
        :relatedStudies   <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn11817821> ;
        :dataStatus       "Under Embargo" ;
        :manifestation    :Plexiform_Neurofibroma ;
        :institutions     "Massachusetts General Hospital" ;
        :studyName        "Whole Body MRI of NF Patients" ;
        rdf:type          :Study .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25881328>
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25881334> ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn6633069> ;
        :manifestation       :Low_Grade_Glioma ;
        :studyLeads          "Nicole Brossier" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://doi.org/10.54464/pc.gr.148704> ;
        :institutions        "Washington University in St. Louis" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999950> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5698493> ;
        :studyName           "Defining the factors that dictate the pattern of glioma formation in NF1" ;
        :fundingAgency       "NTAP" ;
        :initiative          "Francis S. Collins Scholars Program" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :description         "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is a cancer predisposition syndrome caused by a wide spectrum of germline mutations in the NF1 gene. Children with NF1 have a ~20% risk of developing low-grade brain tumors of the optic pathway (optic pathway glioma; OPG) or brainstem (brainstem glioma; BSG). However, it is currently unclear which children with NF1 will develop these brain tumors. Emerging human population-based evidence has raised the possibility that patient-specific risk factors, including infant birth weight and the specific germline NF1 mutation that each child is born with, may modulate the risk of glioma development. High-infant birth weight can be modeled in mice by maternal exposure to a high-fat high-sucrose (HFHS) diet. Based on my preliminary data demonstrating that both germline mutation and maternal HFHS diet increase proliferation and glial differentiation of specific neuroglial progenitor (NPC) populations, I now hypothesize that both disease-intrinsic factors (different germline NF1 gene mutations; Aim 1) and disease-extrinsic factors (maternal HFHS diet; Aim 2) act on spatially-defined populations of NPCs to modulate NF1-glioma penetrance. In this grant, I will determine whether specific germline Nf1 mutations affect the proliferation and differentiation of NPCs from different ventricular surfaces over the course of mouse brain development (Aim 1) and whether maternal HFHS diet exposure modifies these responses (Aim 2). Taken together, these experiments aim to determine whether germline NF1 gene mutations and maternal diet function as risk factors for NF1-glioma formation in different regions of the brain." .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25961160>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22268652> ;
        :institutions        "University of Iowa" ;
        :studyLeads          "Patrick Breheny" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn11681835> ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :studyLeads          "Munir Tanas" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25961164> ;
        :studyLeads          "Jordan Kohlmeyer" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26347297> ;
        :studyName           "Defining the RABL6A-YAP axis in MPNST pathogenesis and therapy" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267323> ;
        :manifestation       :Unknown_Annotation ;
        :description         "MPNSTs represent one of the most aggressive and clinically challenging subtypes of sarcoma. The only known cure for MPNST is complete surgical resection with adjuvant radiation and/or chemotherapy. Unfortunately, surgery is often not feasible due to the location and/or size of the tumors, and traditional radiation and chemotherapy does not reduce patient mortality. NF1 patients are at increased risk of MPNST development compared to the general population due to transformation of plexiform neurofibromas (~one-third), and MPNSTs are the leading cause of death in these patients. Although our understanding of neurofibroma (NF) transformation and MPNST biology has significantly increased in recent years, effective treatment for these deadly tumors is still lacking. My thesis studies centered on understanding the contributions of RABL6A, a potent oncoprotein, to MPNST pathogenesis in an effort to develop meaningful, effective therapies for patients. We demonstrated RABL6A negatively regulates the retinoblastoma (RB1) tumor suppressor, a key pathway known to be disrupted during the transformation of NFs-to-MPNSTs. Further, we and others have found RABL6A promotes MEK signaling, another important pathway driving NF1-associated MPNST development. Gene expression microarray studies suggest RABL6A controls many cancer-promoting pathways, only a few of which have been investigated. While the pre-malignant transition from NF to an intermediate lesion (ANNUBP) appears to occur with few genetic alterations, MPNSTs are highly complex with numerous chromosomal gains and losses. The key alterations required for MPNST development are not known, highlighting a major knowledge gap in the field. Our long-term goal is to define essential molecular events promoting MPNST development to identify new therapeutic targets and establish more effective therapy options for patients. We recently discovered that inhibitors of CDK4/6 (RB1 negative regulator) combined with MEK inhibitors synergistically combats MPNST growth in vivo. The combination dramatically delayed tumor progression and increased mouse survival by more than 3-fold. However, drug resistance inevitably occurred, emphasizing the need for additional targeted therapies. Preliminary data suggest RABL6A is a new regulator of YAP, another oncoprotein whose hyperactivity has recently been implicated in MPNSTs. Further, induction of YAP activity has been linked to CDK4/6 and MEK inhibitor resistance in other cancers. Our objective in this proposal is to elucidate the mechanistic link between RABL6A and YAP in MPNST and evaluate the efficacy of RABL6A-YAP targeted therapy. Our central hypothesis is that RABL6A and YAP act cooperatively to drive MPNST pathogenesis and represent clinically meaningful targets for therapy. In Aim 1, we will define the specific molecular connections and functional importance of RABL6A-YAP signaling in MPNSTs using patient specimens and cell line models. The hypothesis to be tested is that YAP activation by RABL6A drives MPNST pathogenesis. In Aim 2, we will determine the pathobiological significance and therapeutic value of targeting the RABL6A-YAP axis in MPNSTs using genetic editing and de novo tumor models. The hypothesis to be tested is that combination targeting of the RABL6A-YAP axis will be highly synergistic against MPNSTs and abrogate drug resistance. By elucidating the mechanisms and biological relevance of RABL6A-YAP regulation and targeting in MPNSTs, we expect to improve the therapeutic options for patients suffering from this deadly disease." ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :studyLeads          "Dawn Quelle" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26241781> ;
        :initiative          "Young Investigator Award" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn22267330> ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :studyLeads          "Rebecca Dodd" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999248>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5714289> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23651229> ;
        :institutions        "Indiana University School of Medicine" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :studyLeads          "Steven Rhodes" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn23639889> ;
        :studyName           "Targeting the Let7-HMGA2 axis in Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumor Prevention" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :manifestation       :Unknown_Annotation ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999252> ;
        :fundingAgency       "NTAP" ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://doi.org/10.54464/pc.gr.147660> ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn17098100> ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :manifestation       :Plexiform_Neurofibroma ;
        :description         "Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (MPNST) are incurable sarcomas that represent the leading cause of death in NF1 patients. Developing therapeutic strategies to not only treat MPNST, but ultimately to prevent their occurrence is paramount to improve survival, reduce suffering, and alleviate the burden of care for persons with NF1. Our laboratory has developed genetically engineered mouse (GEM) models that accurately phenocopy the development of MPNST from plexiform (PN) and atypical neurofibroma (ANNUBP) precursor tumors, thus allowing for interrogation of interventions that can both treat as well as prevent MPNST. These GEM models have led to the identification of an oncofetal microRNA-protein axis (Let7- HMGA2) that undergoes profound alteration in both murine and human PN/ANNUBP as these tumors progress to MPNST. We hypothesize that Let7-HMGA2 deregulation reprograms broad transcriptional and kinase networks to drive MPNST progression by reactivating programs of embryonic growth in their tumor initiating cells of origin. By isolating this unique population of cancer stem cells from the embryonic nerve roots, we will systematically characterize how Let7-HMGA2 perturbation alters broad networks of protein kinases and transcription factors to drive the aggressive behavior of these tumors. Furthermore, through both genetic and pharmacologic methods, we will establish whether HMGA2 inhibition can treat and/or prevent the progression of MPNST from PN and ANNUBP precursor tumors in vivo. If this is confirmed, we will have identified a targetable pathway to prevent the occurrence of these devastating tumors by controlling and eradicating early clones of disease in a vulnerable population of at risk patients.\")" ;
        :manifestation       :Atypical_Neurofibroma ;
        :initiative          "Francis S. Collins Scholars Program" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn7217935> .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999239>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26438193> ;
        :fundingAgency       "NTAP" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn17100988> ;
        :institutions        "Stanford University" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :description         "Ras/mitogen-activated protein kinase (RMK) pathway is central for brain development and function. In children with neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1), genetic mutations affecting the RMK pathway lead to multisystemic disorder, including measurable effects on behavior and cognition. Mainly, NF1 increases the risk of deficits in attention, hyperactivity, and social skills. While the effects of NF1 on human brain structure and function are evident, their usability as outcome measures is limited. Using high-resolution structural MRI, diffusion-weighted imaging, resting-state fMRI, (neural correlates) and a targeted battery of cognitive-behavioral assessments, we will first assess the neural correlates of, and cognitive-behavioral features associated with attentional and social dysfunction in children with NF1 relative to controls. Then, we will leverage recent advances in brain imaging techniques and computational methods; we will assess the effect of NF1 on the organization and coupling of large-scale structural and functional brain networks. We hypothesize that NF1 is associated with abnormal organization of brain networks implicated in attention and social skills. Our sample will include 30 children with NF1 seen at 5-11 years of age. We will compare them to 30 sex- and age-matched typically developing controls as well as to a (clinical) group of 30 age-matched children with Noonan syndrome (NS). The inclusion of a clinical control group (NS) that is caused by a different genetic mutation related to the RMK pathway will enable us to define a specific profile of brain-based correlates of neurodevelopmental disabilities in children with NF1. The rationale for the proposed project is that defining the brain organization and characterizing the coupling between brain structure and function in NF1 holds promise for providing novel brain-based outcome measures for future clinical interventions in NF1." ;
        :studyLeads          "Tamar Green" ;
        :grantDOI            <https://doi.org/10.54464/pc.gr.147661> ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :studyName           "Towards clinical translation of imaging studies in neurofibromatosis type 1" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26410510> ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999243> ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :manifestation       :Unknown_Annotation ;
        :manifestation       :Unknown_Annotation ;
        :initiative          "Francis S. Collins Scholars Program" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999950>
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn5698493> ;
        :initiative          "Synodos" ;
        :studyLeads          "Antonio Iaverone" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999954> ;
        :manifestation       :Glioma ;
        :description         "The study aims to the characterization of the intra-tumor heterogeneity and microenvironment of glioma occurring in neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1) patients by profiling individual cells using single cell RNA sequencing. We performed individual cell level analyses using single-nucleus RNA-sequencing (Nucseq). Nucseq allows the analysis of frozen tumor specimens, the only currently banked NF1 glioma specimens. We note that profiling of tissue by Nucseq does not require tissue dissociation and has therefore the advantage of preserving the cell types that could be lost during the dissociation process (i.e. more fragile cell types). Single-nucleus molecular profiling allows to dissect the complex architecture underlying glioma subclonal heterogeneity as well as to characterize the tumor microenvironment. We were particularly interested in the identification of subpopulations reflecting neuronal or glial differentiation or their progenitors, as this may uncover the cell-of-origin, cancer stem cell, and potential avenues for differentiation. We evaluated the role of genetic heterogeneity in generating transcriptionally-distinct subpopulations by analyzing mutations (identified in single cell RNA-Seq reads), and chromosomal aberrations (inferred from expression of contiguous sets of genes) to define genetic sub-clones and examine the link between genetics and transcriptional programs. By generating single cell resolution data with minimal technical confounders, we highlighted the oncogenic programs unique to NF1 glioma and candidate vulnerabilities of defined NF1 glioma sub-classes." ;
        :studyName           "Single cell sequencing of glioma in NF1 patients" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :institutions        "Columbia University" ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn6633069> ;
        :dataType            :Raw_Counts ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25881328> ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn21355225>
        :studyLeads          "Hector Salvador" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :description         "Background: Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) is a genetic condition characterized by the development of multiple tumors of the nervous system. The clinical presentation of the disease is variable and related to the type of the inherited constitutional mutation variant in the NF2 gene. The use of antisense oligonucleotides (AONs), acting at RNA level, could be useful to correct the effect of deleterious DNA mutations at the protein level, or to create proteins that still retain partial function from loss-of-function mutated alleles. AONs They could be used to force the skipping of exons carrying truncating mutations when the reading frame is maintained. We have shown that a deep intronic mutation responsible for an aberrant NF2 splicing could be corrected by the use of PMOs and recover Merlin function. Here, we propose to evaluate the use of AONs PMOs to reduce the severity of NF2 truncating and splicing mutations to milder hypomorphic forms in vitro.   Methods: Samples: primary fibroblasts cultures from NF2 patients. Phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomers (PMOs)AONs: We designed specific phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomers (PMOs) targeting four independent disease-causing splicing mutationsvariants, and a pair of PMOs to induce exon skipping of exons 4, 6&7, 8, and 11 of the NF2 gene. Primary fibroblasts carrying nonsense mutations variants were treated with the corresponding pair of PMOs. A dose-response and a time course study were performed to set-up conditions and confirmed the in-frame skipping of the target exons by RT-PCR and Sanger sequencing. Functional analysis: Functional studies were performed from fibroblast cultures untreated and treated at different PMOs concentrations. We measured: 1) Merlin, ERK and their phosphorylated forms by Western Blot; 2) actin cytoskeleton organization; 3) NF2 and YAP1 nuclear translocation capacity by immunofluorescence, and 4) proliferation capacity.   Results: MutationVariant-specific PMOs for splicing mutations: our results showed the PMOs designed for splicing variants located at +/- 15 (or less) from the intron-exon boundary region, were not specific enough and interfered in the correct splicing signaling of both wild-type and mutated alleles. Exon-specific PMOs for truncating mutationsvariants: the effect of the exon skipping on both the mutated and the WT allele, although maintaining the NF2 gene reading frame at cDNA level, caused the complete loss of Merlin. Despite these results, complementary functional analysis are being performed to confirm the absence of Merlin at a cellular level.  Conclusions: PMOs might be optimal for inducing targeted exon skipping without altering gene transcription in cells carrying deep intronic variants, or for the modulation of alternative spliced exons. However, here we show that, in the case of NF2, PMOs may not be useful for correcting splicing when mutations are located in splicing canonical sites, neither for inducing semi-functional Merlin isoforms, since the use of these antisense molecules results in total Merlin loss" ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :studyLeads          "Elisabeth Castellanos" ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn21642010> ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        :institutions        "Germans Trias I Pujol Research Institute (IGTP)" ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999949> ;
        :studyLeads          "Ignacio Blanco" ;
        :studyLeads          "Sandra Bonache" ;
        :initiative          "Drug Discovery Initiative Registered Reports" ;
        :institutions        "Hospital Universitari Germans Trias i Pujol (HUGTP)" ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :studyLeads          "Núria Catasús" ;
        :studyLeads          "Adrià Plana" ;
        :studyLeads          "Isabel Bielsa" ;
        :studyLeads          "Eduard Serra" ;
        :studyName           "Evaluation of the use of antisense oligonucleotides as a personalized therapy for Neurofibromatosis Type 2 patients" ;
        :studyLeads          "Belen García" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_2 .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26238842>
        :manifestation       :Schwannoma ;
        :description         "Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) is a genetic disorder caused by inactivating mutations in the gene encoding the tumor suppressor merlin. NF2 patients develop schwannomas, meningiomas, and ependymomas throughout their life. NF2 patients have few therapeutic options; given the chronic and recurring nature of NF2, careful monitoring and resection radio surgery remains the treatment of choice. Further understanding the drivers of cell proliferation in NF2 and identifying new potential therapeutic targets remains paramount to improve the selectivity of drugs intended for the treatment of these chronic, recurrent tumors. Our group made a fundamental discovery: production of the powerful oxidant peroxynitrite and subsequent tyrosine (Tyr) nitration, an oxidative modification to proteins that occurs in pathological conditions, are essential for NF2 schwannoma cell survival. Excitingly, protein Tyr nitration is very low or absent in healthy cells. Our published and preliminary data posit nitrated proteins as a new category of tumor-directed targets for the development of tumor-directed therapies. We are determined to identify the role of nitrated proteins in the pathogenesis of NF2 and propose Tyr nitrated proteins are a novel therapeutic target. We have identified a number of proteins that are endogenously nitrated in vestibular schwannomas (VS) from NF2 patients. Six of these nitrated proteins are directly involved in signaling pathways dysregulated by the loss of merlin: Galectin 1 (Gal-1), Ras homolog family member A (RhoA), RhoC, Ras related protein 35, 14-3-3, Vimentin and Heat shock protein 90 (Hsp90). We discovered that nitrated Hsp90 supports schwannoma cell survival through a gain-of-function, but we also discovered that Hsp90 is not the only nitrated protein with pro-tumorigenic functions, and these other players remain unidentified. Therefore, the goals of the project are to 1) determine the regulation of signaling pathways by Tyr nitration in general, and Tyr nitrated Hsp90 in particular; and 2) identify the specific nitrated protein(s) that support NF2 tumor cell survival, providing a wealth of novel information and opening the door to the development of new therapies for NF2 treatment." ;
        :initiative          "Young Investigator Award" ;
