<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26238926>
        :description         "Cutaneous (CNF) and plexiform neurofibromas (PNF) are defining features of Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1), accounting for substantial morbidity and mortality across the age spectrum. Although histologically similar, CNFs and PNFs exhibit vastly different clinical behavior as they demonstrate distinct growth rates, anatomic sites of presentation, and malignant potential. The reasons for this disparate clinical behavior are not well explained by genome or transcriptome profiling studies. We recently demonstrated that CNFs and PNFs are epigenetically distinct neoplasms that exhibit genome-wide differences in chromatin conformation and site-specific methylation events (SSMEs) such as differentially methylated regions (DMRs). Collectively, these methylation events altered genes involved in key biological processes, particularly the coupling of inflammatory and RAS signaling. Epigenetic reinforcement of RAS/MKK/p38 was a defining characteristic of CNFs leading to pro-inflammatory signaling and transient receptor potential (TRP) pain receptor regulation, whereas PNFs signaled predominantly through RAS/MEK. This difference is clearly demonstrated in the downstream RAS/p38 signaling effector, COX2.  In the COX2 gene, PTGS2, we identified a DMR near the promoter that was significantly hypomethylated in CNFs compared to PNFs, with the strongest hypomethylation present in symptomatic CNFs. These data confirm that 1) CNFs are epigenetically distinct from PNFs, 2) treatment of CNFs will require targeting signaling pathways outside of or parallel to RAS/MEK such as inflammatory pathways, and 3) RAS signaling fates downstream of NF1 are strongly under epigenetic control. Our goal is to develop effective and sustainable treatments for CNFs, as well as practical diagnostic tools. To do this, we will define how the methylation and chromatin conformation states of key regulatory genes affect CNF growth, symptoms, response to MEK inhibitors and other novel CNF therapies. Our overarching hypothesis is that inflammatory signaling plays a critical role in CNF symptom evolution and tumor growth that is determined both by SSMEs and p38-mediated chromatin conformation changes (i.e. �epigenetic reinforcement�). To test this hypothesis we will establish an inflammatory pain signature by comparing the methylation profiles of symptomatic and asymptomatic CNFs, and in a correlative study determine if treatment with the topical COX2 inhibitor, diclofenac, can reverse this inflammatory pain signature in patients with symptomatic CNFs. At the same time, we will strategically collect additional untreated tumor and normal tissue from patients and unaffected first-degree relatives for patient derived explants (PDEs) that model human CNFs or skin in culture. Using PDEs we will determine how NF1 loss poises the skin microenvironment to support CNF formation and drive pain signaling in response to environmental inflammatory stimuli. We will also evaluate several novel therapeutic targets using CNF PDEs, including MAPK modulators (p38, DUSP, MEK) as well as epigenetic treatments (5-Azacytidine) to determine if they can reverse epigenetic reinforcement of inflammatory pain signaling ex vivo. Finally, we will use the epigenomic data generated in this study as a validation cohort against a CNF/PNF SSME-based biomarker prediction panel assembled from our previous study and several publicly available data sets. Collectively, this work will establish the biological efficacy of topical COX2 inhibition for treatment of CNFs, evaluate novel CNF therapeutic targets preclinically, elucidate how NF1 loss and epigenetic reinforcement of inflammatory signaling supports CNF growth and symptom evolution, and design and test an epigenetic biomarker panel for CNFs. As a result, our proposal will greatly advance CNF diagnosis and treatment for NF1 patients." ;
