        :manifestation       :Unknown_Annotation ;
        :description         "Neural crest derived Schwann cells give rise to neurofibromas, schwannomas, and MPNSTs. Despite their shared embryonic origin, the clinical course of these tumors is quite different. Neurofibromas and schwannomas are benign lesions that are well managed with local therapy while MPNSTs are aggressive tumors that metastasize and have poor outcomes.1 MPNSTs can arise from neurofibromas or schwannomas following malignant transformation in the setting of NF1 loss or ionizing radiation.2�5 NF1 encodes a tumor suppressor that inhibits Ras signaling, a conserved pathway required for development and misactivated in 1/3 of cancers6 including Schwann cell derived tumors.7 However, only a minority of neurofibromas and schwannomas progress to MPNSTs, suggesting additional steps are required. In addition to NF1, MPNSTs harbor mutations in the Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 (PRC2),8�10 a chromatin modifier which regulates gene repression in development and cancer via H3K27 trimethylation.11 My objective is to elucidate how epigenetic mechanisms contribute to malignant transformation in Ras activated tumors." ;
