<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26383473>
        :studyLeads          "Filipp Kulikov" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_2 ;
        :manifestation       :Meningioma ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :studyName           "Exploiting cytotoxic role of nuclear Rac1 to develop targeted antitumor therapy of NF2-associated tumor" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn2343195> ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :description         "Neurofibromatosis type II (NF2) is a genetic disorder characterized by the development of multiple tumors in central nervous system. NF2 is an autosomal dominant genetic disease caused by mutation appearance in NF2 gene, i.e. if a child, a boy or a girl, is unlucky to born with such mutation then the disease will develop with a probability of almost 100%. There is no any specific treatment for NF2 just only non-specific radiotherapy and surgery but sometimes that methods are useless due to remote localization of tumor. Our proposal is to use statins and bisphosphonates for treating NF2 patients because the mechanism of their action is based on specific features of NF2 tumors. Furthermore we propose to use statins and bisphophonates jointly because in our previous studies we have established that the mechanism of action of each compound affect the same process in cell but in different stages and both compounds slowed down the growth of NF2-tumors in preclinical studies. These data demonstrates that joint using of statins and bisphosphonates have ability to enhance effects of each other that may lead to decreasing size of NF2-associated tumors. In our studies already approved drugs are used which safety is proved (simvastatin, lovastatin belonging to statin, zolderonic acid belonging to bisphosphonates). Our proposal suggests to use difference in activity of the protein Rac1 between cells of NF2-associated tumors and normal cells as target for specific pharmaceutical therapy. Rac1 activity is increased in NF2 tumor cells compared to normal cells. It is a pivotal step for development of this approach which will allow to move on preclinical stuides with laboratory animals. We hope that results obtained in our studies could be used for cure NF2-patients." ;
