<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26410510>
        :studyName           "Neural Underpinnings of Attention in Children with NF1" ;
        :institutions        "University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee" ;
        :studyLeads          "Sara K. Pardej" ;
        :studyStatus         "Active" ;
        :diseaseFocus        :Neurofibromatosis_Type_1 ;
        :initiative          "Young Investigator Award" ;
        :dataStatus          "Under Embargo" ;
        :studyFileviewId     <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26410514> ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26438193> ;
        :fundingAgency       "CTF" ;
        rdf:type             :Study ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn25999239> ;
        :accessRequirements  "The data from this study is currently under embargo. Please contact the principal investigator for access to the data." ;
        :description         "Attention difficulties are one of the most apparent aspects of the cognitive phenotype of neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1), yet very little is known about the underlying neural mechanisms of attention in children with NF1. Children with NF1 have difficulties with various aspects of attention, including sustained, divided, shifting, visual, and auditory (Casnar & Klein-Tasman, 2017; Isenberg et al., 2013; North et al., 2002).  While some children with NF1 show attention difficulties that meet criteria for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD), there are also children whose difficulties do not meet that threshold but who nevertheless still demonstrate marked difficulties with sustained attention (Mautner et al., 2001) and planning (Hyman et al., 2005). Research in other populations, such as ADHD, has begun elucidating specific biomarkers of attention difficulties using electroencephalography (EEG) Event Related Potentials (ERPs). EEG is a relatively affordable and non-invasive imaging technique that can be used to further delineate neural mechanisms of attention difficulties. However, very few studies have used EEG methodology to examine the neural underpinnings of these attention difficulties in children with NF1. The goal of this research is to demonstrate the feasibility and promise of EEG approaches with school age children with NF1 to identify potential biomarkers of attention problems. While there is considerable variability in the behavioral phenotype of NF1, attention problems are commonly identified. There is a dearth of research about the neural underpinnings of these challenges, especially with school age children.   There is a need to identify biomarkers of attention difficulties in children with neurofibromatosis type 1 to effectively track the impact of medical and psychosocial interventions that affect attention in NF1. Research shows that many youth with NF1 experience attention problems (Huijbregts et al., 2010; Templer et al., 2013), which are evident on performance-based, caregiver and teacher report measures (Casnar & Klein-Tasman, 2017), however, the neural underpinnings of attention problems in children with NF1 have not yet been sufficiently characterized. This may be important for identifying potential biomarkers of attention difficulties, and eventually neural underpinnings of attention in children with NF1 by understanding the impact of interventions (including medications) on attention and related brain function. Unique EEG patterns may provide researchers with an NF1-specific index of attention difficulties at the neural level rather than based on questionnaire and neuropsychological task performance measures. Further, there is evidence from other populations that changes in the neural underpinnings may in fact be evident prior to behavioral changes; for example, in Social Phobia, reduced activity in the amygdala and limbic regions of the brain may be a “prerequisite” for behavioral improvement (Ribeiro Porto et al., 2009). Such evidence is also present in studies of children with ADHD, which has led a task force to recommend continued research into neurophysiological methods, such as ERPs, as biomarkers in ADHD (Thome et al., 2012). And, indeed, research about the utility of ERPs as biomarkers has also been expanded to other populations that are affected by ADHD, such as Tuberous Sclerosis Complex (Moavero et al., 2020). Thus, extending this work to NF1, which has considerable overlap with ADHD, is warranted.  We will examine attention in 7 through 11-year-olds with NF1 using a multi-method approach. This includes caregiver questionnaires about attention, performance-based measures of attention administered by trained staff evaluators, and Event Related Potentials (ERPs) ascertained through electroencephalography (EEG). We will use a Flanker task and a Go/Nogo task, which are known to elicit our components of interest: N2, P3, and ERN. The N2 and P3 components are indices of visual selective attention to salient stimuli. Error-Related Negativity (ERN) appears in response to making an error of inhibition. Our sample will include 20 children diagnosed with NF1, 20 children diagnosed with idiopathic Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD), and 20 unaffected children without both NF1 and ADHD. The groups will be matched by age and sex. We have chosen a narrow age range because developmental EEG studies suggest that there is considerable age-related variability in some ERP components; the broad age range of studies to date of children with NF1 might obscure developmental effects.   Aim 1 is to examine group differences in ERP measures of attention. Our aim is to identify neural underpinnings of attention problems in school age children with NF1 in comparison to a clinical contrast group of children with ADHD and a control group of children without both NF1 and ADHD. We hypothesize that compared to same-aged unaffected children, children with NF1 will show a reduced, and potentially delayed, N2 and P3 to incongruent Flanker and Nogo trials, and a reduced error-related negativity (ERN) following errors on both tasks. We also hypothesize that compared to same-aged children with ADHD, children with NF1 will show different patterns of N2, P3, and ERN activity. Aim 2 is to examine relations between specific ERP components and behavioral indicators of attention problems in children with NF1. We will examine the relations between components (N2, P3, and ERN) that show group differences with behavioral indicators of attention problems, including both performance-based and caregiver-report measures. We will also examine any differences in the patterns of relations between groups. We hypothesize that reduced amplitude and latency will be related to poorer attention on both performance-based and caregiver-report measures." ;
        :relatedStudies      <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn17100988> .

<https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26438193>
        :relatedStudies   <https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn26410510> ;
